{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And for your beast and for the wild animal</b> — if the wild animal eats, the domesticated animal all the more so, since its sustenance is upon you. And what does the verse teach by saying \"and for your beast\"? It juxtaposes the domesticated animal to the wild animal: as long as the wild animal eats from the field, you feed your domesticated animal from the house; when the wild animal is finished from the field, finish off your domesticated animal from the house — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:7), from Torat Kohanim (chapter 1, halakhah 8).\n\nNow the Rav did not explain what this \"finishing off\" (כִּלּוּי) is. Its meaning is that one must finish off the produce after its season has passed by declaring it ownerless, and this is the בִּעוּר of the Sabbatical year that the Sages mentioned everywhere. And the matter is not that the produce after the time of בִּעוּר is forbidden for benefit and for eating, such that one would be obligated to destroy it. For the Sages did not enumerate in the Mishnah (end of Temurah) the produce of the Sabbatical year among those that must be burned or among those that must be buried. Rather, one is required only to remove them from his domain and to declare them ownerless to the poor and to any person, in the sense of \"I have cleared out the consecrated portion from the house\" (Deuteronomy 26:13).\n\nAnd thus we learned (Shevi'it 9:8): The poor eat after the בִּעוּר but not the rich — the words of Rabbi Yehudah. Rabbi Yosei says: Both the poor and the rich eat after the בִּעוּר. And the meaning of \"poor\" is: all who gathered the produce from others' fields from the ownerless; and \"rich\" — the owners of the fields themselves who gathered them from their own fields when they declared them ownerless. And Rabbi Yehudah forbade it to them by rabbinic decree on account of suspicion, while Rabbi Yosei permits it, and the halakhah follows his words.\n\nAnd we learned in the Tosefta (Shevi'it 8:1–4): At first, emissaries of the court would go around to the openings of the towns. Anyone who brought produce into their hands — they would take it from him and give him food for three meals, and the remainder they would bring into a storehouse in the city. When the season of figs arrived, the emissaries of the court would hire laborers and pick them and press them into cakes of dried figs, and so on. When the season of olives arrived, the emissaries of the court would hire laborers and harvest them and press them in the oil press and gather them into jars and bring them into the storehouse in the city. When the season of grapes arrived, the emissaries of the court would hire laborers and harvest them and tread them in the winepress and gather them into jars and bring them into the storehouse in the city. And he would distribute from them on the eves of Sabbaths, each and every one according to his household. When the time of בִּעוּר arrived, the poor eat after the בִּעוּר but not the rich — the words of Rabbi Yehudah. Rabbi Yosei says: Both the poor and the rich eat after the בִּעוּר. Rabbi Shimon says: The rich eat from the storehouse after the בִּעוּר. One who has produce of the Sabbatical year and the time of בִּעוּר has arrived — he distributes from it to his neighbors and to his relatives and to his acquaintances, and he takes it out and places it at the opening of his house and says: Our brothers, the House of Israel, all who need to take — let them come and take. And he brings it back inside his house, and he continues eating until the hour when they are finished. All this is taught in the Tosefta.\n\nAnd we have learned explicitly that בִּעוּר is nothing but removing the produce from one's domain and declaring it ownerless. And they enacted ordinances by their own authority: at first the court would make a storehouse in every city. From the time the produce began to emerge, they would take it from the hand of the one who brought it and bring it into the storehouse. And when the time for gathering that species arrived — for example, when the time of harvest and the season of vintage arrived — the court would hire laborers and harvest and pick and gather all of that species, and tread and press in the winepress and in the oil press as in other years, and place it in their storehouse. And these produce items gathered into the court's storehouse do not require בִּעוּר, since they are already cleared from the house. And both poor and rich are permitted after the בִּעוּר to receive from the court's hand and to eat them. And all this ordinance and the labor of the court is on account of suspicion, lest they come to withhold them or to conduct commerce with them.\n\nAnd when there is no storehouse in the city and no court, and the produce is in the hand of the one who gathered it from the ownerless — he is required to clear it from his house at the time of בִּעוּר and to declare it ownerless at the opening of his house, and they may continue eating forever. And this is \"the Sabbatical year that prohibits its species for a certain period\" (Shevi'it 7:7), through בִּעוּר, as is mentioned in tractate Nedarim (Nedarim 58), because it has a permitting factor through בִּעוּר from one's house.\n\nAnd I found language of Rashi, who wrote in tractate Pesachim (52a, s.v. mishum): And this is their בִּעוּר — that one declares them ownerless in a place trodden by the feet of people and animals. Perhaps the Rav thought that one must declare them ownerless also to the wild animal and to the domesticated animal, in order to fulfill in them \"and the poor of your people shall eat\" (Exodus 23:11) and \"and for your beast and for the wild animal that is in your land\" (here), and he went beyond the plain requirement. In any event, they are not prohibited in eating at all.\n\nNow we have already learned in the Tosefta (Shevi'it 7:3) that בִּעוּר of the Sabbatical year is like the בִּעוּר of second tithe — not that there is a distinction between them in the prohibition of eating. And the Rav, Rabbi Moshe (Mishneh Torah, Laws of Shemittah and Yovel 7:3), and many of the Sages hold that בִּעוּר prohibits entirely, and they require burning or scattering and casting to the wind or casting into the sea. But this is not so, as has been explained. Rather, if one retained them in his house after the בִּעוּר in order to eat them, they are entirely forbidden for eating. And this is \"the Sabbatical year that prohibits by giving flavor after the בִּעוּר\" (Shevi'it 7:7), for which there is no permitting factor. And it is possible that this prohibition too is by rabbinic decree. And perhaps even the בִּעוּר in its entirety is a stringency of rabbinic origin, and the baraitot taught in Torat Kohanim concerning the matter of בִּעוּר are merely an asmakhta of rabbinic authority.\n\nAnd along this line one may well explain the Mishnah of one who presses three pressings in a single vat, and so on (Shevi'it 9:5), and likewise the Mishnah of the three lands for the Sabbatical year (ibid. 6:1), which teaches: All that the returnees from Babylon held from the Land of Israel up to Keziv — is neither eaten nor worked; all that the returnees from Egypt held from Keziv to the River and to Amanah — is eaten but not worked. They were lenient regarding eating after the בִּעוּר more than regarding working the land.\n\nAnd it is mentioned in the Yerushalmi (Shevi'it, end of chapter 9): A certain man was suspected regarding the Sabbatical year. He said to his wife: \"Bring out the challah.\" She said to him: \"That man is suspected regarding the Sabbatical year, and you say 'Bring out the challah'?\" He said to her: \"Challah is a matter of Torah; the Sabbatical year is rabbinic in the days of Rabban Gamliel and his court.\" That worthless man was suspected of withholding produce of the Sabbatical year and eating it after the בִּעוּר, and he said that the prohibition of eating tevel is from the Torah, while the בִּעוּר and the prohibition of eating produce of the Sabbatical year after the בִּעוּר are from Rabban Gamliel and his court — or that the בִּעוּר is from the Torah and the prohibition of eating is by rabbinic decree, and he was not concerned except lest he eat Torah-prohibited things. And since the Sages mentioned his words, it appears that they are true.\n\nOr perhaps that wicked man held according to the words of Rabbi Shimon, who said (Mo'ed Katan 2b): The verse speaks of two Sabbatical years, and the Sabbatical year in this era is rabbinic even though challah is from the Torah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The Aramaic anecdote from Yerushalmi is rendered into English while preserving its original argumentative structure; the speaker's wife addresses him as 'that man' (הַהוּא גַּבְרָא), which is preserved literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:11.320647181Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}